# `d[i][0] * d[i][1]` over a list of lists of numbers: the row is a list that the turn itself finds. In the typed copy of the loop it is asked, in the turn, what a list in front of the loop is asked:
# whether it is as made and which kind its storage has; where an answer fails the turn begins again in the other copy. Behind the answers `d[i]` is read once and both elements are raw numbers.
# The kind is decided once a row: an arm for storage of doubles, one for int32, one for boxed values (a literal of whole numbers is born so), each with the reads and what leans on them; no read asks the kind again.
# In a nest the inner loop is built twice as well, though the list of rows was proven by the loop around it.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = TypeTest {{*}} testedType=Array<number>
# CHECK: = IsPristine {{*}} builtinClass=Array aspects=[Storage, Names]
# CHECK: = HasArrayStorage {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double level=Proven
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double level=Proven
# CHECK: = Mul {{*}} form=Numbers
# CHECK: = HasArrayStorage {{*}} arrayStorage=Int32
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Int32 level=Proven
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Int32 level=Proven
# CHECK: = HasArrayStorage {{*}} arrayStorage=Contiguous
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Contiguous level=Proven
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Contiguous level=Proven
# RUN(ONCE): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# ONCE-NOT: admitsInt32Storage
# RUN(NEST): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# NEST-LABEL: code f2:Body
# NEST: = HasArrayStorage {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# NEST: = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double level=Proven
# -- source: a-row-that-the-turn-finds-is-asked-in-the-turn-what-a-list-in-front-is-asked.ts
